<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Xue</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.) · capital Xue</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An old Ren-clan state in southern Shandong that ended as the fief of Lord Mengchang, the most famous patron of retainers in the Warring States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Xue was ruled by the Ren clan, who traced their line to Xi Zhong, legendary inventor of the cart and a minister of the Xia. The Zhou recognised it as a minor state with its capital near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengzhou">Tengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-lu">State of Lu</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-song">State of Song</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Xue played a small part in the alliances of the Spring and Autumn period, sending its rulers to interstate covenants led by Qi and Jin. Its walled city, whose remains survive south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengzhou">Tengzhou</a>, was large for so minor a state, suggesting a prosperous agricultural and trading centre on the eastern plain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a> absorbed Xue in the 4th century BCE and granted it as a fief to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Ying. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-mengchang">Tian Wen</a>, Lord Mengchang, ruled from Xue and kept thousands of retainers there, making the name famous long after the old state had gone.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1046 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:38.89%;width:14.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xue</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xue</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-684&amp;focus=state-of-xue" aria-label="State of Xue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7377 993 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Xue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7969 1105h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7697 1249h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7697 1249h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-684&amp;focus=state-of-xue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

